    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos</a> had split his army before the battle, and the Byzantine reserve under Andronikos Doukas withdrew to the camp instead of covering the retreat, which left the rest of the line exposed.</li><li>Modern scholars estimate that Byzantine losses were relatively low and that many units fought elsewhere within a few months. The lasting damage came from the civil war that followed, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> used to their advantage.</li><li>Later Byzantine writers called Manzikert a disaster and greatly exaggerated the numbers of troops and casualties; Delbrück thought the battle's importance exaggerated.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>It was the only time a Byzantine emperor became the prisoner of a Muslim commander, and the first time since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> that a Roman emperor was captured alive by an enemy force.</p></aside>
    </section>

