    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1053</span><span class="rf-text">Constantine IX disbands the &quot;Iberian Army&quot;, which Skylitzes puts at 50,000 men</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1064</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> attacks the theme of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and takes Ani after a siege of 25 days</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1068</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos IV Diogenes</a> takes power and begins two major campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and their Mirdasid allies</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1071</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos</a>'s army reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Theodosiopolis</a> and the march continues towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1071</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos</a> takes Manzikert and his army is defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> nearby</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1071</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos</a> is released after a week, loses three battles to the Doukas family, and is deposed and blinded</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1077</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> establish their capital at Nikaea</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1095</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> begins as a western response to the Byzantine emperor's call for help</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-manzikert" data-v="205619c668" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Byzantines form up and march out</b> The Byzantine army gathered into battle formation and marched on the Seljuk positions, with Bryennios on the left, Theodore Alyates on the right and the emperor in the centre. Andronikos Doukas held the reserve in the rear, and the Seljuks stood in a crescent about four kilometres away.</li><li><b>Horse archers draw the crescent round them</b> Seljuk horse archers shot at the Byzantines as they drew closer. The centre of the crescent kept falling back while the wings moved round to surround the advancing troops.</li><li><b>The Byzantines take the camp, but the wings fray</b> The Byzantine centre pressed on despite the arrows and captured Alp Arslan's camp by the end of the afternoon. The wings, where the arrows did most damage, almost broke up as units tried to force a pitched battle, and the Seljuk cavalry simply disengaged when challenged.</li><li><b>The withdrawal is ordered and misunderstood</b> With the Seljuks avoiding battle, Romanos ordered a withdrawal. The right wing misunderstood the order, and Doukas ignored it and marched his reserve straight back to the camp outside Manzikert instead of covering the retreat.</li><li><b>The Seljuks attack and the right wing breaks</b> With the Byzantines confused, the Seljuks attacked. The right wing was almost at once routed, its men thinking they had been betrayed.</li><li><b>The left wing is routed and the centre encircled</b> The left wing under Bryennios held out a little longer but was soon routed. The Seljuks encircled what remained of the centre, including the emperor and the Varangian Guard, and Romanos was wounded and taken prisoner.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes.jpg" alt="Romanos IV Diogenes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos IV Diogenes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Romanus_IV_coin_crop.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown / Photography: CNG coins, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> had remained strong in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, but it began to decline under the militarily incompetent Constantine IX Monomachos and again under Constantine X Doukas. A brief two-year period of reform under Isaac I Komnenos only delayed the decay of the army. About 1053 Constantine IX disbanded what the historian John Skylitzes called the "Iberian Army", of 50,000 men. Skylitzes' contemporaries Michael Attaleiates and Kekaumenos agree that this did catastrophic harm to the empire's eastern defences. A truce with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> lasted until 1064, when a large army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> attacked the theme of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and took Ani after a siege of 25 days.</p><p>In 1068 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos IV Diogenes</a> took power and, after some military reforms, conducted two major campaigns against the Seljuks and their Mirdasid allies. He retook Hierapolis in 1068 and chased Turkoman armies that were raiding across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, with limited success, but he could not win a decisive victory in 1069. In 1069 or 1070 Manuel Komnenos led an expedition and was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sebasteia</a> by Arisighi, Alp Arslan's brother-in-law, who then joined the Byzantines and gave them information about Seljuk politics and tactics.</p><p>Alp Arslan failed to capture Edessa after a lengthy siege and turned to besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. Romanos saw an opportunity to lead a large army into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and recover the lost fortresses before the Seljuks could respond.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Manzikert&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Prelude:</p><ul><li>Ian Heath, Byzantine Armies, 886–1118 (Osprey, 1979)</li><li>John Haldon, The Byzantine Wars: Battles and Campaigns of the Byzantine Era (Tempus, 2001)</li><li>David Nicolle, Manzikerk 1071. The Breaking of Byzantium (Bloomsbury, 2013)</li><li>Carole Hillenbrand, Turkish Myth and Muslim Symbol: The Battle of Manzikert (Edinburgh University Press, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos</a> went Andronikos Doukas, son of his rival John Doukas. The army had about 5,000 professional troops from the western provinces and probably about as many from the eastern provinces, drawn from the central field army, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagma-military">tagmata</a>. The rear guard under Doukas was largely made up of the private retinues and peasant levies of the border lords. The host also held 500 Frankish and Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> under Roussel de Bailleul, Uz, Pecheneg and Bulgarian mercenaries, infantry under the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, a Georgian and Armenian contingent and some, but not all, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a>, around 40,000 men in total. The provincial troops had declined in number because the government diverted funds to mercenaries, who were judged less likely to be involved in politics and could be disbanded after use.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> was already in the area with allies and 30,000 cavalry from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. The Marwanid emir Nizam al-Din ibn Nasr al-Dawla sent a force of Kurdish fighters estimated at 10,000. Alp Arslan's scouts knew where Romanos was, while Romanos did not know where his opponent was.</p><p>Romanos then ordered Joseph Tarchaniotes to take some of the regular troops and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, to Khliat, while he marched on Manzikert. This left about 20,000 men in each half. What became of Tarchaniotes's force is unknown.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos</a>, unaware that Tarchaniotes was gone, took Manzikert on 23 August, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> answered with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a>. The next day foraging parties under Bryennios found the main Seljuk force and fell back, and the cavalry Romanos sent under the Armenian general Basilakes was routed and Basilakes captured. On 25 August some of Romanos's Turkic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> met their Seljuk kin and deserted, and Romanos rejected a Seljuk peace emissary.</p><p>On the morning of 26 August <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a>, in a white robe like a funeral shroud, made a speech to his army. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> formed up with Bryennios on the left, Theodore Alyates on the right, the emperor in the centre and Andronikos Doukas leading the reserve in the rear. The Seljuks stood in a crescent about four kilometres away. Their horse archers attacked as the Byzantines advanced, the centre of the crescent giving way while the wings moved round the Byzantine troops. The Byzantines captured Alp Arslan's camp by the end of the afternoon, but the wings almost broke up when units tried to force a pitched battle and the Seljuk cavalry simply disengaged.</p><p>Romanos ordered a withdrawal by nightfall. The right wing misunderstood it, and Doukas ignored the order to cover the retreat and marched to the camp. The Seljuks attacked, routed the right wing, then the left under Bryennios, and encircled the remnants of the centre, including the emperor and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a>. Romanos was wounded and taken prisoner.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Manzikert&#8221; cites where it covers Battle, Captivity of Romanos Diogenes:</p><ul><li>David Nicolle, Manzikerk 1071. The Breaking of Byzantium (Bloomsbury, 2013)</li><li>Carole Hillenbrand, Turkish Myth and Muslim Symbol: The Battle of Manzikert (Edinburgh University Press, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos</a> was brought before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a>, the sultan at first did not believe that the bloodied man was the emperor. After learning who he was, he placed his boot on Romanos's neck and made him kiss the ground, a traditional symbolic gesture, then treated him with considerable kindness and offered again the peace terms he had offered before the battle. Romanos stayed a captive for a week and ate at the sultan's table while terms were agreed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, Edessa, Hierapolis and Manzikert were to be surrendered. The sultan's demand of 10 million gold pieces as ransom was judged too high and was reduced to 1.5 million as an initial payment followed by 360,000 a year, with a marriage alliance between the sultan's son and Romanos's daughter.</p><p>Doukas had escaped without casualties and went back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where he led a coup against Romanos and proclaimed his cousin Michael VII <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basileus">basileus</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> did not pursue the fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> nor retake Manzikert at this point. All the Byzantine commanders survived and most prisoners were later released. The most serious material loss was the emperor's baggage train.</p><p>Romanos was defeated three times in battle by the Doukas family, deposed, blinded and exiled to the island of Proti, and died soon after of an infection caused by the blinding.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Manzikert&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath:</p><ul><li>David Nicolle, Manzikerk 1071. The Breaking of Byzantium (Bloomsbury, 2013)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Much of what is known comes from Byzantine writers, among them Michael Attaleiates, John Skylitzes and Kekaumenos, who were contemporaries or near contemporaries, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-komnene">Anna Komnene</a>, writing a few decades after the battle. Islamic sources add details, and Ibn al-Adim reports what <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos</a> said before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a>, that he blamed raids by Rashid al-Dawla Mahmud into Byzantine territory for his interventions in Muslim territories.</p><p>The sources disagree in places. According to Islamic sources Alp Arslan smashed the army sent off with Tarchaniotes, but Byzantine sources do not mention any such encounter, and Attaleiates suggests Tarchaniotes fled at the sight of the sultan, an event that seems unlikely given the general's reputation. Some authors suppose the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were the first to flee, while others say Armenian infantry resisted stoutly and did not abandon the emperor.</p><p>Later Byzantine historians looked back on the day as a disaster and the start of the empire's decline, and later sources greatly exaggerated the numbers of troops and casualties. Modern scholars have revised the older picture of a massacre. Andrew Holt, in a 2022 anthology, argues that Romanos was largely to blame, citing his decision to give Andronikos so large a command, his division of his forces on poor intelligence and the poor leadership and discipline of his troops.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In simplest terms the defeat meant the loss of the empire's Anatolian heartland. John Julius Norwich calls it "its death blow" and the greatest disaster in the empire's seven and a half centuries, and Steven Runciman calls it the most decisive disaster in Byzantine history. Thomas Asbridge says historians no longer consider it an utterly cataclysmic reversal, though still a stinging setback. Delbrück considers its importance exaggerated, but the evidence shows it left the empire unable to put an effective army into the field for many years.</p><p>The battle was the first link in a chain: intrigues for the throne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos</a>'s fate, and Roussel de Bailleul's attempt to carve out a kingdom in Galatia with 3,000 Frankish, Norman and German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. By 1080 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> had gained an area of 78,000 square kilometres, and they made Nikaea their capital in 1077. It took three decades of internal strife before Alexius I restored stability. The First Crusade of 1095 began as a western response to the Byzantine emperor's call for help after the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-myriokephalon">Battle of Myriokephalon</a> has been compared to it.</p><p>Norman soldiers who had served in Byzantine armies spread tales of the defeat that likely inspired an episode in the Chanson de Roland. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> the Çamlıca Mosque's minarets span 107.1 metres, a measurement that refers to 1071, and the 1000th anniversary of the battle is one of the targets announced in 2018 by the ruling AKP.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Manzikert on 26 August 1071 the Seljuk army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> routed a Byzantine host led by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos IV Diogenes</a>, who was captured. Byzantine losses were relatively low by modern estimates, but civil war followed and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> gained much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</em></p>

